              "id": "glog-2bf555eb-64ef-444a-bc60-7518a1c6011e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPotential Interface Binding Vulnerability in Python refers to a situation where a server is configured to listen on all interfaces (0.0.0.0), which can expose the server to the internet. This can lead to unauthorized access and potential exploitation of any vulnerabilities present in the server.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to bind the server to localhost (127.0.0.1) or a specific IP address that is not publicly accessible. This will limit the exposure of the server to the internet and reduce the risk of unauthorized access.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```python\nserver.run(\"127.0.0.1\", port=args.port)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided does not specify the library being used. However, it seems to be using a server object which could be from a library like Flask. In that case, the Flask library would be required.\n\n```python\npip install flask\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-933: OWASP Top Ten 2013 Category A5 - Security Misconfiguration](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/933.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
